# Stained-glass room fully submerged in a walled flat-world pool. Water
# emits its boundary faces against translucent neighbours exactly like
# opaque-order glass (mesh_vis_logic.hpp liquid_boundary only culls
# waterlogged cells), so a dry-backed glass wall or ceiling shows tinted
# water through it — the underwater feel from inside. Companion to
# glass_in_water.rds. Screenshots must be
# eyeball-compared; the asserts only pin the world state.
# flags 9 = lighting off + fog off so the underwater view isn't murk.
wait_idle 20 6000
# Basin
fill 1 1 1 16 10 1 STONE
fill 1 1 16 16 10 16 STONE
fill 1 1 2 1 10 15 STONE
fill 16 1 2 16 10 15 STONE
# Glass room x5..11 y2..6 z5..11 built as six shells so the interior
# (untouched flat-world air) is never written by fill.
fill 5 2 5 11 2 11 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
fill 5 6 5 11 6 11 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
fill 5 3 5 5 5 11 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
fill 11 3 5 11 5 11 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
fill 6 3 5 10 5 5 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
fill 6 3 11 10 5 11 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
# Water around and above the room; the interior column stays air.
fill 2 1 2 4 8 15 WATER
fill 12 1 2 15 8 15 WATER
fill 5 1 2 11 8 4 WATER
fill 5 1 12 11 8 15 WATER
fill 5 7 5 11 8 11 WATER
wait 10
wait_idle 20 6000
assert_block 8 6 8 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
assert_block 8 4 8 AIR
assert_block 8 7 8 WATER
flags 9
# Inside the dry room, looking up through the glass wall/ceiling at the
# water — side and bottom faces should be drawn.
camera 8.5 3.5 8.5 180 -35
wait 30
screenshot inside
echo glass_room_water done
